# Ported from mwdmwd/ghidra-c28x (Apache-2.0): the `:^instruction` prefix-wrapper
# pattern for the C28x hardware repeats and the `rpt_active` / `rptb_flag` /
# `rpt_phase` context-bit scheme. See THIRD-PARTY.md.
#
# SLEIGH loop models for RPT and RPTB. The loops live in p-code, so the
# DECOMPILER sees them -- which the Java EmulateInstructionStateModifier never
# provided. RPTB is fully retired from that callback and emulates from here.
#
# RPT is not, and cannot be: the wrappers are armed by `globalset`, and Ghidra's
# emulator applies a globalset commit one instruction too late (the value
# written for the target address only becomes visible after that instruction has
# already been decoded and executed). RPTB targets the block-end address, far
# enough ahead that the commit has landed. RPT targets inst_next -- the very
# next instruction -- so the base constructor always wins the match under
# emulation and the wrapper below never runs (RPTC is never decremented). The
# RPT arm/re-issue therefore stays in postExecuteCallback for EMULATION only;
# this file remains the model the decompiler sees. Disassembly is unaffected in
# both cases. See docs/EMULATION.md "Why RPT still needs the modifier".
#
# HOW IT WORKS
# ------------
# The C28x's zero-overhead repeats have an implicit loop-back that a single
# instruction's p-code cannot express. The `:^instruction` prefix wrapper
# handles this by re-executing the wrapped instruction under decoded p-code
# control: the RPT body arms `rpt_active=1` on the address of the instruction
# to be repeated, the wrapper fires on that address, builds the inner
# instruction, and either falls through (counter exhausted) or decrements and
# jumps back to inst_start.
#
# PHASE-BIT PARTITION -- non-obvious, load-bearing
# ------------------------------------------------
# `:^instruction is X & instruction { ... build instruction; ... }` compiles to
# a variant of every base constructor whose pattern is (X AND base_pattern). If
# a base constructor imposes NO context constraint that contradicts X, the
# variant and the base overlap -- both match at addresses where X holds.
# `sleigh -l` reports this as "Constructor patterns cannot be distinguished",
# and at runtime the plain base wins, silently making the wrapper a no-op.
#
# Every shipped Ghidra processor that uses `:^instruction` (avr8, 8051, ARM,
# Hexagon) fixes this by giving base constructors a positive-phase constraint
# that the wrapper's pattern negates. We do the same: every top-level `:MNEMONIC`
# constructor requires `& rpt_phase=1`, the pspec seeds `rpt_phase=1` as the
# ram-space default, and RPT / RPTB globalset `rpt_phase=0` on the wrapped
# address alongside `rpt_active` / `rptb_flag`. The wrapper's pattern then
# includes `rpt_phase=0`, so it matches uniquely there; its local
# `[ rpt_phase=1; ]` action flips the phase back for the inner `build
# instruction`, allowing the base to match on re-parse.
#
# Neither RPT nor RPTB nests on the C28x (single RPTC / single RB), so the
# 1-bit context flags + register-backed counters are hardware-accurate.

# RPT wrapper: the repeated instruction runs once via `build instruction`, then
# loops back RPTC additional times. Total executions = RPTC + 1, matching the
# C28x definition (RPT #N repeats N+1 times). `rptb_flag=0` in the pattern is
# for mutual exclusion with the RPTB wrapper below (both wrappers gate on
# rpt_phase=0; without the exclusion sleigh -l reports "cannot be distinguished"
# because the ambiguity checker considers the theoretical case where both bits
# hold, even though RPT and RPTB cannot arm the same address in practice).
:^instruction  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & instruction  [ rpt_phase=1; ] {
    build instruction;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto inst_next;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto inst_start;
}

# RPTB wrapper: fires at the block-end address (globalset from the RPTB body's
# rptb_end sub-table). Falls through when RB_RC is exhausted; otherwise
# decrements RB_RC and jumps back to RB_RSTART (the first instruction of the
# block, i.e. inst_next of the RPTB itself). RB_RA cleared on the falling-
# through pass, matching hardware. `rpt_active=0` for mutual exclusion vs the
# RPT wrapper above.
:^instruction  is rpt_phase=0 & rptb_flag=1 & rpt_active=0 & instruction  [ rpt_phase=1; ] {
    build instruction;
    if (RB_RC == 0) goto <done>;
    RB_RC = RB_RC - 1;
    goto [RB_RSTART];
  <done>
    RB_RA = 0;
}

# =============================================================================
# REPEATED PROGRAM TRANSFERS -- the program-pointer shadow
# =============================================================================
# Adapted from mwdmwd/ghidra-c28x (Apache-2.0), "Model repeated program-transfer
# shadows". See THIRD-PARTY.md.
#
# SPRU430F, PREAD loc16,*XAR7, Repeat: "When repeated, the *XAR7 program-memory
# address is copied to an internal shadow register and the address is post-
# incremented by 1 during each repetition." Two consequences, and a block copy
# needs BOTH:
#
#   1. the program-side pointer WALKS FORWARD across repetitions -- otherwise
#      `RPT || PREAD` degenerates from a copy into a fill, writing the first word
#      N times;
#   2. the ARCHITECTURAL pointer is UNCHANGED when the repeat ends -- the
#      increment happened on the shadow. TI's .cinit walk depends on this: it
#      advances XAR7 by hand afterwards (`XAR7 += AR1+1`), so a model that also
#      advanced XAR7 would double-count and desynchronise the walk from the
#      second record onward.
#
# The generic RPT wrapper above cannot express either one. It re-executes the
# whole instruction from inst_start, and every re-execution re-reads XAR7 (or the
# pma immediate, or AL) from scratch. These forms therefore replace the wrapper
# for the repeated program transfers: they hold the shadow in a SLEIGH unique and
# run the repeat themselves, `build`ing the loc16 operand once per transfer so its
# pre/post update still fires per iteration -- which is what makes the DATA side
# of `PREAD *XAR6++,*XAR7` walk while the PROGRAM side walks independently.
#
# Iteration count matches the wrapper (and the hardware): the body runs once, then
# RPTC more times, so `RPT #N` transfers N+1 words and leaves RPTC at 0.
#
# PATTERN PRECEDENCE. Each form carries the wrapper's full context constraint
# (rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0) PLUS its opcode, so its pattern is a
# strict subset of the wrapper's `instruction` union and sleigh resolves it ahead
# of the wrapper instead of colliding with it. The wrapper's `[ rpt_phase=1; ]`
# action is deliberately NOT copied: nothing here re-parses a whole instruction,
# and the loc16 sub-table is gated on ctx_AMODE, not on the phase bit.
#
# N/Z follow the same destination-conditioned rule as the un-repeated forms
# (SPRU430F: flags only when the destination is an accumulator half), which is why
# @AH / @AL are separate constructors here too. On a repeat they are refreshed
# after every transfer, so what survives the repeat is the flag state of the LAST
# word -- and TI's .cinit walk reads exactly that.
#
# The C2xLP `X*` forms below apply the 0x3F program page, like the un-repeated
# constructors they mirror -- SPRU430F forces the upper 6 bits of the 22-bit program
# address for the whole family. See the xpma* sub-tables in tms320c28x_flow.sinc,
# and note the NON-X counterparts force 0x00 instead.
#
# EMULATION -- these DO run there, and that needed handling. Contrary to the note
# above (measured 2026-09 on Ghidra 12.1.2), the armed context IS in effect at the
# repeated address under emulation: `RPT #2 || ADDB ACC,#1` steps with RPTC going
# 2 -> 1 -> 0, and only the wrapper's p-code decrements RPTC. That is harmless for
# the wrapper, whose loop-back is an external `goto inst_start` -- the same address
# the state modifier re-issues, so one execution happens per step either way. It is
# NOT harmless for the INTERNAL loop below, which completes the entire repeat inside
# one instruction and then gets re-issued on top: measured as a 3-word block copy
# advancing its destination pointer 5 times. TMS320C28xEmulateInstructionStateModifier
# resolves it by zeroing RPTC when it arms one of these opcodes, collapsing each issue
# to a single transfer so the modifier keeps driving the count and stepping the *XAR7
# shadow. The loop written here is what the DECOMPILER reads. EmuPreadRepeatTest pins
# the emulator half; on the disassembly side a `RPT #2 || PREAD *XAR6++,*XAR7` yields
# p-code that copies the shadow out of XAR7, rebuilds `*XAR6++` per iteration, and
# never writes XAR7.

# --- PREAD loc16,*XAR7  (0x24) : shadow the *XAR7 SOURCE -----------------------
:PREAD "@AH", "*XAR7"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0x24 & ctx_AMODE=0 & loc_full8=0b10101000  {
    local shadow:4 = XAR7;
  <pread_ah>
    AH = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AH);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <pread_ah_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <pread_ah>;
  <pread_ah_done>
}
:PREAD "@AL", "*XAR7"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0x24 & ctx_AMODE=0 & loc_full8=0b10101001  {
    local shadow:4 = XAR7;
  <pread_al>
    AL = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AL);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <pread_al_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <pread_al>;
  <pread_al_done>
}
:PREAD loc16, "*XAR7"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0x24 & loc16  {
    local shadow:4 = XAR7;
  <pread_loc>
    build loc16;
    local v:2 = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    loc16 = v;
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <pread_loc_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <pread_loc>;
  <pread_loc_done>
}

# --- PWRITE *XAR7,loc16  (0x26) : shadow the *XAR7 DESTINATION -----------------
:PWRITE "*XAR7", loc16  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0x26 & loc16  {
    local shadow:4 = XAR7;
  <pwrite_loc>
    build loc16;
    local v:2 = loc16;
    *[ram]:2 shadow = v;
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <pwrite_loc_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <pwrite_loc>;
  <pwrite_loc_done>
}

# --- XPREAD loc16,*(pma)  (0xAC) : shadow the pma IMMEDIATE --------------------
:XPREAD "@AH", "*("xpmaaddr")"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0xAC & ctx_AMODE=0 & loc_full8=0b10101000 ; imm16 & xpmaaddr  {
    local shadow:4 = xpmaaddr;
  <xpread_imm_ah>
    AH = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AH);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_imm_ah_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_imm_ah>;
  <xpread_imm_ah_done>
}
:XPREAD "@AL", "*("xpmaaddr")"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0xAC & ctx_AMODE=0 & loc_full8=0b10101001 ; imm16 & xpmaaddr  {
    local shadow:4 = xpmaaddr;
  <xpread_imm_al>
    AL = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AL);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_imm_al_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_imm_al>;
  <xpread_imm_al_done>
}
:XPREAD loc16, "*("xpmaaddr")"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op_hi8=0xAC & loc16 ; imm16 & xpmaaddr  {
    local shadow:4 = xpmaaddr;
  <xpread_imm_loc>
    build loc16;
    local v:2 = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    loc16 = v;
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_imm_loc_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_imm_loc>;
  <xpread_imm_loc_done>
}

# --- XPREAD loc16,*AL / XPWRITE *A,loc16  (0x563c / 0x563d) --------------------
# The shadow starts at AL and steps; AL itself is untouched by the progression.
:XPREAD "@AH", "*AL"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & ctx_AMODE=0 & op16=0x563c ; loc_full8=0b10101000  {
    local shadow:4 = 0x3f0000 | zext(AL);
  <xpread_al_ah>
    AH = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AH);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_al_ah_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_al_ah>;
  <xpread_al_ah_done>
}
:XPREAD "@AL", "*AL"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & ctx_AMODE=0 & op16=0x563c ; loc_full8=0b10101001  {
    local shadow:4 = 0x3f0000 | zext(AL);
  <xpread_al_al>
    AL = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    setNZ16(AL);
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_al_al_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_al_al>;
  <xpread_al_al_done>
}
:XPREAD loc16, "*AL"  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op16=0x563c ; loc16  {
    local shadow:4 = 0x3f0000 | zext(AL);
  <xpread_al_loc>
    build loc16;
    local v:2 = *[ram]:2 shadow;
    loc16 = v;
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpread_al_loc_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpread_al_loc>;
  <xpread_al_loc_done>
}
:XPWRITE "*A", loc16  is rpt_phase=0 & rpt_active=1 & rptb_flag=0 & op16=0x563d ; loc16  {
    local shadow:4 = 0x3f0000 | zext(AL);
  <xpwrite_al_loc>
    build loc16;
    local v:2 = loc16;
    *[ram]:2 shadow = v;
    shadow = shadow + 1;
    if (RPTC == 0) goto <xpwrite_al_loc_done>;
    RPTC = RPTC - 1;
    goto <xpwrite_al_loc>;
  <xpwrite_al_loc_done>
}
